FIX THE REPLAY (DoD 1 and 3). Edit scripts/tests/runtime-requirements.test.mjs only with the session's own editing tool (Claude Edit/Write, or apply_patch on a codex exec seat). Never use a shell write or a subagent.

Replace the Docker CI smoke harness at :28-59. Drop the `dockerStub` PATH script, the mkdtemp bin dir, the `t.after` cleanup and the PATH prepend. Replay the step as `spawnSync(bash, ['-e', '-c', smokeStubs + commands], ...)`.

Keep exactly:
- the two `Docker <stage> image satisfies the daemon Node minimum` tests;
- the step extraction (the 10-space `run: |` lines);
- the bash choice (`C:/Program Files/Git/bin/bash.exe` on win32, else /bin/bash) and `cwd: root`;
- `timeout: 10000` (never raise it);
- `DOCKER_HOST: 'tcp://127.0.0.1:1'`, the fail-fast guard in case a real docker is ever reached;
- the `MOE_DOCKER_SMOKE_MODE` and `MOE_EXPECTED_DOCKER_VERSION` env names;
- `assert.ifError(result.error)` and the title `Docker CI smoke ${mode} has exit ${exitCode}`.
Remove the imports this leaves unused: mkdtempSync, mkdirSync, rmSync, writeFileSync and tmpdir.

Error handling:
- `assert.ifError(result.error)` still fails a case by name on a spawn failure or the 10 s cap;
- the docker stub's catch-all fails any subcommand it does not model, with exit 97 and a stderr line naming the call;
- every assertion message carries the step's full stdout+stderr.

smokeStubs is this template literal. It holds no `${` and no backslash; keep it that way:

docker() {
  case "$1" in
    build|logs|rm) ;;
    run)
      if [ "$2" = -d ]; then echo moe-smoke-container; return; fi
      case "$MOE_DOCKER_SMOKE_MODE" in
        fail) return 43 ;;
        mismatch) echo not-the-package-version ;;
        *) echo "$MOE_EXPECTED_DOCKER_VERSION" ;;
      esac ;;
    port) echo 127.0.0.1:49153 ;;
    inspect) if [ "$MOE_DOCKER_SMOKE_MODE" = healthcheck-fails ]; then echo 'exit 3'; else echo 'exit 0'; fi ;;
    exec) shift 2; "$@" ;;
    *) echo "docker stub: unexpected docker $*" >&2; return 97 ;;
  esac
}
curl() {
  case "$*" in *' http://127.0.0.1:49153/health') ;; *) return 7 ;; esac
  [ "$MOE_DOCKER_SMOKE_MODE" != no-health ] || return 7
  local status=healthy project
  project=$(pwd -W 2>/dev/null || pwd)
  case "$MOE_DOCKER_SMOKE_MODE" in
    other-project) project=/elsewhere ;;
    unhealthy) status=starting ;;
  esac
  printf '{"status":"%s","projectPath":"%s"}' "$status" "$project"
}
sleep() { :; }

Put a short comment above it giving two reasons. Both were measured at plan time under Windows node.exe 24.16 with Git for Windows' bash.
1. Functions, not PATH files. That bash puts /mingw64/bin and /usr/bin ahead of the PATH we pass, so `type -P curl sleep` gave /mingw64/bin/curl and /usr/bin/sleep. PATH stubs with those names (the hunt's FIX DIRECTION) never run on the windows-latest leg (mem:gotcha-git-bash-stub-path-and-msys-sparse). A function also costs no process, so the 30-try loop takes milliseconds.
2. `pwd -W`. Git Bash hands node.exe the step's EXPECTED="$PWD" converted to D:/..., while a stub's $PWD stays /d/.... A stub answering $PWD failed the match case on Windows. On other systems `pwd -W` errors and pwd answers.

Stay bash 3.2-safe for macOS /bin/bash: no [[ =~ ]], ${v,,}, mapfile or local -n.

This step is done when the existing fail, mismatch and match cases pass, with match well under 1 s.

A vetted copy of the whole end state is at /mnt/d/moe-scratch/arch-7e862808/cand/runtime-requirements.test.mjs. It is a scratch file: read it, never cp it into the tree.
